\section{Hardened Zero-Copy Cryptographic Hardware Controller}
\label{sec:hardware-controller}

\subsection{Design Overview}
The AFXE hardware controller is conceived as a security-critical boundary
module that binds the real-time hydrodynamic telemetry stream directly to
cryptographic lattice evolution within device-resident memory. Its primary
design goals are:
\begin{itemize}
\item zero-copy ingestion of simulation telemetry into hardware registers,
\item elimination of disk I/O in the cryptographic pipeline,
\item deterministic coupling between thermodynamic entropy and lattice state,
\item strict detection of lattice divergence or numerical instability.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Memory-Mapped Telemetry Interface}
Let $N$ denote the number of fluid nodes or entropy samples in the telemetry
buffer. The controller allocates a device-resident buffer
\[
\mathbf{E} \in \mathbb{R}^N
\]
for entropy-bearing telemetry and an associated cryptographic stream buffer
\[
\mathbf{C} \in \mathbb{R}^N
\]
for encrypted output. Host-to-device transfer is performed under a zero-disk
constraint, i.e.\ no intermediate persistent storage.

\subsection{Chaotic Lattice State Variable}
The hardware controller maintains a scalar lattice state
\[
x_{\mathrm{hw}} \in (0,1),
\]
initialized to a nontrivial constant (e.g.\ an irrational anchor). Given a
telemetry vector $\mathbf{E}$, the controller computes a global entropy
aggregate
\[
\bar{E} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N E_i.
\]

\subsection{Tent Map Integration in Hardware}
The lattice update is defined by a perturbed tent map
\[
x_{\mathrm{hw}}^{+}
= 1 - 2\big|x_{\mathrm{hw}} - \tfrac{1}{2}\big|
+ \epsilon \bar{E} \mod 1,
\]
where $\epsilon>0$ is a coupling coefficient. The controller enforces:
\begin{itemize}
\item boundedness: $x_{\mathrm{hw}}^{+} \in (0,1)$,
\item non-divergence: $x_{\mathrm{hw}}^{+} \not\in \{\pm\infty,\mathrm{NaN}\}$,
\item strict error signaling on any violation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Security and Sovereignty Semantics}
Conceptually, the hardware controller:
\begin{enumerate}
\item binds the cryptographic lattice to the physical state of the fluid
simulation via $\bar{E}$,
\item prevents any decoupling of keys from physics by eliminating disk-based
intermediates,
\item enforces a deterministic yet chaotic evolution of $x_{\mathrm{hw}}$,
\item exposes a sovereignty boundary: only entities with access to the live
telemetry stream can reproduce the lattice state.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Operational Status}
Under nominal operation, the controller reports:
\[
\text{Bit-Loss Ratio} = 0.00\%,
\]
indicating no detected cryptographic decoupling or hardware-level data loss
within the validated stress envelope.